  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/1908.01474v2</id>
    <title>Integral representation of probabilities in Kingman coalescent</title>
    <updated>2019-08-14T10:04:14Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.01474v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/1908.01474v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Kingman Coalescent was first proposed by Kingman [7] in population genetics to describe population's genealogical structure. Now it becomes a bench-mark model for coalescent process. Extensive studies have been conducted on Kingman coalescent. In particular, its explicit finite time distribution was obtained by Tavaré [12]. However, very few people use this explicit distribution to do analysis for it is an intractable infinite series. In this article, we are going to establish a complex integral representation for the finite time distribution, then we use steepest descent method to analyze this integral representation to obtain local central limit theorem at small time regime.</summary>
    <category term="math.PR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2019-08-05T05:36:15Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>11 pages, 2 figures</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="math.PR"/>
    <author>
      <name>Youzhou Zhou</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/1501.03963v1</id>
    <title>A Spectral decomposition for the Bolthausen-Sznitman coalescent and the Kingman coalescent</title>
    <updated>2015-01-16T12:19:25Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1501.03963v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/1501.03963v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We consider both the Bolthausen-Sznitman and the Kingman coalescent restricted to the partitions of $\{1, \ldots, n\}.$ Spectral decompositions of the corresponding generators are derived. As an application we obtain a formula for the Green's functions and a short derivation of the well-known formula for the transition probabilities of the Bolthausen-Sznitman coalescent.</summary>
    <category term="math.PR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2015-01-16T12:19:25Z</published>
